      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for this. it's a decent start, but it feels a bit too brief for a study guide. can you expand on it? maybe add a bit more detail to the actual steps so they really grasp the mechanics. also, i just checked the score on this draft and it was pretty low on those signposting words, so try to weave those in more naturally so the tool picks them up. just make sure it still actually makes sense for a student reading it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course, I'd be happy to help! Mitosis plays a crucial role in maintaining our health by allowing the growth and repair of organisms. You can think of it as a cell getting dressed for a party: each day you grow a little, and every broken bone gets repaired; all thanks to this vital process. Let's dive a little deeper into each step, which typically unfolds over several hours, though this depends on the plant or animal. Ready to take a peek at the mechanism of mitosis?\n\nIn the first phase, prophase, the chromosomes, which contain our hereditary material, become visible and the centromeres, where sister chromatids are linked, start to condense. Simultaneously, the nuclear envelope begins to break apart, signaling that the cell is preparing for division.\n\nFollowing this, we enter metaphase, where the chromosomes snap into place at the cell's equator, much like a tug-of-war rope stretching straight across the center of a field. Each side is balanced, ensuring each new cell will receive an equal amount of genetic material.\n\nThe channeling proceeds into anaphase, when the sister chromatids are split and pulled to opposite ends of the cell. It's like gathering everything in your room and splitting it evenly",
      "source": "target",
